WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.075
Now the risks, stated plainly,
with how to manage each one.

2
00:00:04.075 --> 00:00:06.513
First, cryptographic validation.

3
00:00:06.513 --> 00:00:09.875
There's no active FIPS
140 certificate today.

4
00:00:09.875 --> 00:00:15.519
The 140-2 certificates went Historical
with the September 21 sunset,

5
00:00:15.519 --> 00:00:18.787
and four 140-3 modules are in NIST review.

6
00:00:18.787 --> 00:00:20.963
BlueCat holds an active certificate.

7
00:00:20.963 --> 00:00:25.809
If your system requires validated
cryptography at award, make go-live depend

8
00:00:25.809 --> 00:00:29.974
on the issued certificate, or carry
the gap as a POA&M item with a date.

9
00:00:29.974 --> 00:00:33.590
Ask Infoblox which NIOS versions
the pending modules cover,

10
00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:36.988
because a certificate for the
wrong version doesn't help.

11
00:00:36.988 --> 00:00:43.262
Second, there's no current NIAP
certificate; NIOS 9.0 is in evaluation.

12
00:00:43.262 --> 00:00:46.757
And Infoblox isn't on the
CDM Approved Products List,

13
00:00:46.757 --> 00:00:49.113
which is closed to new submissions.

14
00:00:49.113 --> 00:00:54.012
Track the NIAP evaluation, and
don't promise anyone APL status.

15
00:00:54.012 --> 00:00:56.013
Third, vulnerabilities.

16
00:00:56.013 --> 00:01:02.553
NVD lists four NIOS CVEs from February
2025 rated between 9.1 and 9.8,

17
00:01:02.553 --> 00:01:09.925
including improper authentication for
Grids, and a remote-code-execution

18
00:01:09.925 --> 00:01:15.513
deserialization flaw,
CVE-2025-61880, from February 2026.

19
00:01:15.513 --> 00:01:20.851
None of the Infoblox CVEs is in CISA's
Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog.

20
00:01:20.851 --> 00:01:25.488
Write the patch cadence and the Long-Term
Support train into the ATO plan,

21
00:01:25.488 --> 00:01:29.275
and keep the Grid management
plane off untrusted networks.

22
00:01:29.275 --> 00:01:31.325
Fourth, the BIND dependency.

23
00:01:31.325 --> 00:01:35.461
NIOS and NIOS-X run on
BIND, and NIOS-X updates in

24
00:01:35.461 --> 00:01:40.250
September 2026 shipped to
address lists of BIND CVEs.

25
00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:48.975
On October eighth CISA added an old BIND
flaw, CVE-2015-5477, to the KEV catalog.

26
00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:54.193
Ask Infoblox for its BIND advisory SLA
and a written attestation that current

27
00:01:54.193 --> 00:01:57.405
releases aren't affected
by KEV-listed BIND CVEs,

28
00:01:57.405 --> 00:01:59.813
and track ISC's advisories yourself.

29
00:01:59.813 --> 00:02:03.787
Fifth, the SaaS ceiling is
Moderate, and reuse is zero.

30
00:02:03.787 --> 00:02:10.149
Use agency-operated vNIOS for High or
DoD systems, and budget a full ATO.

31
00:02:10.149 --> 00:02:19.110
Sources: NIST CMVP certificates 4531,
4535, 5506 and modules-in-process list;

32
00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:25.180
NIAP PCL; CISA CDM APL; NVD
CVE API (keyword Infoblox);

33
00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:34.920
CISA KEV catalog 2026.10.21; Infoblox
Cloud release notes; FedRAMP products.json.
